To classify a story into one of the three genres- fairy tale, urban legend or horror story, we can use a machine learning algorithm called the Naive Bayes Classifier. The Naive Bayes Classifier is a probabilistic algorithm that uses Bayes' theorem to classify data into different categories. Here's the C++ code to classify a given story:
```
#include <iostream>
#include <fstream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>
#include <map>
#include <cmath>
using namespace std;
// Function to tokenize a string
vector<string> tokenize(string str, string delimiters) {
    vector<string> tokens;
    size_t pos = 0;
    string token;
    while ((pos = str.find_first_of(delimiters)) != string::npos) {
        token = str.substr(0, pos);
        if (token.length() > 0) {
            tokens.push_back(token);
        }
        str.erase(0, pos + 1);
    }
    if (str.length() > 0) {
        tokens.push_back(str);
    }
    return tokens;
}
// Function to train the classifier
void trainClassifier(map<string, map<string, int>>& wordCounts, map<string, int>& genreCounts, string filename) {
    ifstream file(filename);
    if (file.is_open()) {
        string line;
        while (getline(file, line)) {
            vector<string> tokens = tokenize(line, " ,.;:\"\'");
            if (tokens.size() >= 2) {
                string genre = tokens[0];
                genreCounts[genre]++;
                for (size_t i = 1; i < tokens.size(); i++) {
                    wordCounts[genre][tokens[i]]++;
                }
            }
        }
        file.close();
    }
}
// Function to classify a story
string classifyStory(map<string, map<string, int>>& wordCounts, map<string, int>& genreCounts, string story) {
    vector<string> tokens = tokenize(story, " ,.;:\"\'");
    double maxProb = -INFINITY;
    string maxGenre = "";
    for (auto& genreCount : genreCounts) {
        double prob = log((double)genreCount.second / genreCounts.size());
        for (auto& token : tokens) {
            prob += log((double)(wordCounts[genreCount.first][token] + 1) / (genreCount.second + 2));
        }
        if (prob > maxProb) {
            maxProb = prob;
            maxGenre = genreCount.first;
        }
    }
    return maxGenre;
}
int main() {
    // Train the classifier
    map<string, map<string, int>> wordCounts;
    map<string, int> genreCounts;
    trainClassifier(wordCounts, genreCounts, "training_data.txt");
    // Classify a story
    string story = "In the dead of night, a group of teenagers decided to explore an abandoned mansion. As they made their way through the dusty halls, strange things began to happen. Doors slammed shut on their own and creepy whispers echoed through the rooms. Suddenly, they found themselves trapped and being hunted by an unknown entity. Will they be able to escape before it's too late?";
    string genre = classifyStory(wordCounts, genreCounts, story);
    cout << "The story is classified as: " << genre << endl;
    return 0;
}
```
In this code, we first define a function `tokenize` to tokenize a given string into individual words. We then define a function `trainClassifier` to train the Naive Bayes Classifier on a given training dataset. The training dataset is assumed to be in a text file named "training_data.txt", where each line contains a story followed by its genre (either "fairy tale", "urban legend" or "horror story").
The `trainClassifier` function reads each line of the training dataset, tokenizes the story into individual words, and updates the word counts and genre counts accordingly. The word counts are stored in a nested map `wordCounts`, where the outer map maps each genre to its corresponding inner map that maps each word to its count in the genre. The genre counts are stored in a map `genreCounts` that maps each genre to its count in the dataset.
We then define a function `classifyStory` to classify a given story into one of the three genres. The function tokenizes the story into individual words, calculates the log probability of the story belonging to each genre using the Naive Bayes formula, and returns the genre with the highest probability.
In the `main` function, we first train the classifier on the training dataset using the `trainClassifier` function. We then classify a sample story using the `classifyStory` function and print the result to the console.

