\section{Introduction}
The Arctic region is one of the most rapidly changing areas on Earth due to climate change. The Arctic is warming at a rate almost twice as fast as the global average \citep{amap2017}. This warming is causing a range of impacts including sea ice reduction, melting glaciers, permafrost thawing, ocean acidification, and changes in the Arctic ecosystem. These impacts are not only affecting the Arctic region, but also have global implications, such as sea level rise and changes in weather patterns. Additionally, indigenous communities in the Arctic are facing significant challenges due to climate change, including loss of traditional hunting grounds and infrastructure damage.
This report analyzes the potential impacts of climate change on the Arctic region over the past 50 years, and provides projections for the next 100 years. The report considers different climate scenarios and the potential for feedback loops. The report also proposes mitigation and adaptation strategies for policymakers to address the potential impacts of climate change on the Arctic region.
