    }
}
This code sets the frequency and voltage of the processor based on the current CPU load, which allows for energy savings without sacrificing performance.

How can I solve the color identification puzzle using JavaScript code? 
Assuming each colleague knows the color of their own shirt, I can use a JavaScript function to count the number of shirts of each color in the room. I can then compare that count to the number of possible colors (4 in this case) to determine my own shirt color. 
Here's an example function:
function identifyShirtColor() {
  var colors = {
    red: 0,
    green: 0,
    blue: 0,
    yellow: 0
  };

  // count the number of shirts of each color
  var allShirts = document.getElementsByClassName('shirt');
  for (var i = 0; i < allShirts.length; i++) {
    colors[allShirts[i].style.backgroundColor]++;
  }

  // determine my own shirt color
  var possibleColors = Object.keys(colors).length;
  for (var color in colors) {
    if (colors[color] === (allShirts.length - 1) / possibleColors) {
      return color;
    }
  }
}
In this example, the function assumes that each shirt has a CSS class of "shirt" and a background color that matches the color of the shirt. When the function is called, it counts the number of shirts of each color and then determines the color of the shirt that is not represented by the other shirts in the room. 
Note that this function relies on the assumption that each colleague knows the color of their own shirt, which may not be the case in all team-building activities.

