Sure, here's a function in Java that finds the most repeated element in an array:
```java
public static int findMostRepeatedElement(int[] arr) {
    int n = arr.length;
    int maxCount = 0;
    int maxElement = arr[0];
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        int count = 1;
        for (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {
            if (arr[i] == arr[j])
                count++;
        }
        if (count > maxCount) {
            maxCount = count;
            maxElement = arr[i];
        }
    }
    return maxElement;
}
```
To modify this function to return all the elements that occur more than 50% of the time in the array, we can use a HashMap to keep track of the frequency of each element. Here's the modified function:
```java
public static List<Integer> findFrequentElements(int[] arr) {
    int n = arr.length;
    Map<Integer, Integer> freqMap = new HashMap<>();
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        int count = freqMap.getOrDefault(arr[i], 0);
        freqMap.put(arr[i], count + 1);
    }
    List<Integer> frequentElements = new ArrayList<>();
    int threshold = n / 2;
    for (Map.Entry<Integer, Integer> entry : freqMap.entrySet()) {
        if (entry.getValue() > threshold) {
            frequentElements.add(entry.getKey());
        }
    }
    frequentElements.sort((a, b) -> freqMap.get(b) - freqMap.get(a));
    return frequentElements;
}
```
This function returns a list of all the elements that occur more than 50% of the time in the array, sorted in descending order based on their frequency of occurrence.

