\section{Impact of Climate Change on Food Security}
\subsection{Crop Failure}
Climate change has led to extreme weather patterns, such as droughts, floods, and heatwaves, which have significantly impacted crop production. According to the United Nations, climate change has already reduced crop yields by up to 10 percent globally, and this trend is expected to continue. In addition, the rise in temperature has led to an increase in pests and diseases, further reducing crop yields.
\subsection{Disrupted Food Systems}
Climate change has disrupted food systems, leading to food insecurity in many regions. Extreme weather patterns have affected transportation, storage, and distribution of food, leading to food shortages and price spikes. In addition, climate change has led to a decline in the quality and quantity of freshwater resources, which are essential for agriculture.
\subsection{Impact on Marine Ecosystems}
Climate change has also adversely affected ocean currents, leading to negative implications for marine ecosystems. The rise in temperature has led to coral bleaching, which has affected the diversity and productivity of marine ecosystems. In addition, the increase in carbon dioxide levels has led to ocean acidification, which has further impacted marine life.
\section{Consequences of Inaction}
If effective measures are not taken to mitigate the impact of climate change, up to 122 million people may be pushed into extreme poverty by 2030. In addition, food insecurity will continue to rise, leading to social and political instability in many regions. The adverse effects of climate change on marine ecosystems will also have significant economic consequences, affecting industries such as fisheries and tourism.
\section{Plan of Action}
Immediate intervention is required to mitigate climate change's impact and ensure sustainable food systems for future generations. The following are some of the actions that need to be taken:
