The key indicators of a successful genetic sequencing experiment can vary depending on the type of experiment being performed. However, some common indicators of success include:
1. High quality of sequencing data with low error rates
2. Sufficient coverage depth of the genome or targeted regions
3. High accuracy of base calling and assembly
4. Minimal contamination or artifacts
5. Consistency and reproducibility of results
Here is an example program that takes in genetic data as input and returns true if the experiment meets the criteria for success, and false otherwise:
```
function isSequencingSuccessful(data) {
  let success = true;

  // Check for data quality
  if (data.errorRate > 0.1) {
    success = false;
  }
  if (data.coverageDepth < 30) {
    success = false;
  }
  if (data.baseCallAccuracy < 0.9) {
    success = false;
  }

  // Check for contamination
  if (data.contamination > 0.05) {
    success = false;
  }

  // Check for consistency
  if (!data.isConsistent) {
    success = false;
  }

  return success;
}
```
This is just an example program, and the specific criteria for success may vary depending on the experiment being performed. It is important to consult with experts in the field to determine the appropriate criteria for success.

